problem: In terminal popups coming
kernel:unregister_netdevice: waiting for enp2s0d1.113 to become free. Usage count = 3863
 kernel:unregister_netdevice: waiting for enp2s0d1.113 to become free. Usage count = 3863
 kernel:unregister_netdevice: waiting for enp2s0d1.113 to become free. Usage count = 3863
 kernel:unregister_netdevice: waiting for enp2s0d1.113 to become free. Usage count = 3863

The two commands just flip it:

Command	What it does
sed -i 's/^\*\.emerg/#*.emerg/' /etc/rsyslog.conf	Adds #, turning popups OFF (use now, to stop the spam)
sed -i 's/^#\*\.emerg/*.emerg/' /etc/rsyslog.conf	Removes #, turning popups ON (use after the reboot)

After either one, run:

bash
systemctl restart rsyslog


systemctl stop rsyslog   #, turning popups OFF (use now, to stop the spam)

OK, you can get networking working without a reboot by creating a new VLAN interface with a different name. The old enp2s0d1.113 will stay stuck in the background (the log messages will continue), but the new one can carry your traffic.
Important: some of these commands may hang if the kernel is still locked. Open a second SSH session (or console) first, so you have a backup if one terminal freezes.

1. Get your IP settings from the old config
bash
cat /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp2s0d1.113

Note the IPADDR, PREFIX (or NETMASK), and GATEWAY values.

2. Make sure the physical port is up
bash
ip link set enp2s0d1 up
ip link show enp2s0d1

You should see state UP.

3. Create the new VLAN interface
Use the same VLAN ID (113) with a different name:
bash
ip link add link enp2s0d1 name vlan113 type vlan id 113
ip addr add <IPADDR>/<PREFIX> dev vlan113
ip link set vlan113 up

Example, if your IP is 10.10.113.20/24:

bash
ip addr add 10.10.113.20/24 dev vlan113
4. Add the gateway route (only if this VLAN had the default gateway)
bash
ip route add default via <GATEWAY> dev vlan113


Things to know
Don’t run ifup or ifdown on enp2s0d1.113, and don’t restart the network service. That can hang or make things worse.
This setup is temporary. It disappears on the next reboot, and at that point the original enp2s0d1.113 config will work normally again.
If step 3 hangs, the kernel lock is blocking all network changes. In that case there’s no way to fix it without a reboot.
To stop the popup messages meanwhile, use the sed + systemctl restart rsyslog step from earlier.


